Substructure restrictions allow the inclusion and exclusion of certain molecular substructures, encoded in SMARTS [44]. Each substructure is searched independently, thus overlapping substructures can also be considered. This option is particularly useful for cases where detailed information about a parent substance is known (e.g. transformation product, metabolite elucidation), or complementary substructure information is available from elsewhere (e.g. MS2Analyzer [45] or other MS classifiers [13]). Candidates containing certain substructures can either be included and/or excluded prior to fragmentation, or scored differently. To calculate a score, the number of matches in the inclusion or exclusion list containing n substructures are added per candidate as given in Eq. 3 (where \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
