				\begin{document}$$\omega _\mathrm{PNP + PPC} = 1$$\end{document}ωPNP+PPC=1). The results, shown in Table 3, indicate that the PubChem results can be improved further, to 343 top 1 ranks (73 %). This is a drastic improvement from the performance of both original fragmenters alone, with CFM-ID alone yielding between 10 and 12 % top 1 hits (expected rank) in their original publication [16] with an older PubChem, the combination of both fragmenters alone yielding 15 % (expected rank) here. These combined results are also drastically better than the latest in silico fragmentation results just published for CSI:FingerID. Dührkop et al. [18] investigated each individual fragmenter currently available and compared the results with the CSI:FingerID. Despite using different data and settings to those here, their results on the Agilent dataset indicated that MetFrag2010 and CFM-ID achieved 9 and 12 % top 1 (expected) ranks, which are reasonably comparable with the results presented above. FingerID [15] achieved 19.6 %, while CSI:FingerID achieved 39 % top 1 results, which is a dramatic improvement over the other fragmenters. Since the external information boosted the top 1 ranks to 73 % for MetFrag2.2 plus CFM-ID, one could speculate that the combination of CSI:FingerID, MetFrag2.2 and CFM-ID would result in an even greater performance.

